form<pre><code>
&lt;form method="post" action="FormulairePost.aspx"&gt;
</code></pre> HTML tag&lt;form name="..." method="..." action="..."&gt;...&lt;/form&gt;attributesname="frmexample": form namemethod="..." : method used by the browser to send the values collected in the form to the web serveraction="..." : URL to which the values collected in the form will be sent.A web form is enclosed within the tags &lt;form&gt;...&lt;/form&gt;. The form can have a name (name="xx"). This applies to all controls found within a form. The purpose of a form is to collect information entered by the user via the keyboard or mouse and send it to a web server URL. Which one? The one referenced in the action="URL" attribute. If this attribute is missing, the information will be sent to the URL of the document in which the form is located. A web client can use two different methods called POST and GET to send data to a web server. The method="method" attribute, where method is set to GET or POST, in the &lt;form&gt; tag tells the browser which method to use to send the information collected in the form to the URL specified by the action="URL" attribute. When the method attribute is not specified, the GET method is used by default.
